    {
      "author_association": "COLLABORATOR",
      "body": "We investigated parallelizing BPE training in SentencePiece, prototyping a Hugging Face (HF) style parallel merge approach and analyzing YouTokenToMe's (YTTM) statically partitioned design. \nOur benchmarks on a 19.3MB corpus (~180k unique words, vocab 32,000) show that **parallelizing BPE training does not provide a meaningful speedup and can cause slowdowns:**\n*   **1T Baseline**: 6.86s\n*   **4T (Parallel Threshold 1000)**: 6.30s (~1.09x speedup)*\n*   **4T (Parallel Threshold 10)**: 8.01s (~0.86x speedup - slowdown)\n*\\*Note: The ~9% speedup is due to parallel sentence normalization during initialization (already in SentencePiece), not the BPE loop itself. The BPE parallelization contribution was <1%. \"Threshold\" is the minimum pair frequency required to trigger parallel execution.*\n#### Key Findings\n1.  **Amdahl's Law Constraint**: The parallelizable merge step only accounts for **~24%** of BPE training time in SentencePiece. The remaining **76%** (priority queue updates) is inherently sequential, limiting the maximum theoretical speedup to $\\approx 1.3\\text{x}$.\n2.  **Thread Overhead**: Thread scheduling and synchronization overhead (~160 us per merge) outweighs parallel gains for the long tail of rare merges, causing slowdowns at lower thresholds.\n3.  **HF & YTTM Parallel Limits**: \n    *   **HF-style**: Replicates HF's `Rayon` strategy of parallelizing a single pair's merge. It shares the same sequential PQ bottleneck.\n    *   **YTTM-style & Scalability**: YTTM achieves sub-linear scaling because its BPE-specific monolithic architecture (compact arrays, custom hash maps, split queues) minimizes the sequential fraction ($S$) of the loop. Because SentencePiece relies on heavier generic structures, its sequential fraction remains too high (~76%) to benefit from YTTM's parallel design without a complete rewrite of the core BPE structures.",
      "created_at": "2026-06-16T07:18:14Z",
      "html_url": "https://github.com/google/sentencepiece/issues/366#issuecomment-4715960101",
      "id": 4715960101,
